    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis examines the current endangerment level of Wenzhounese, a Chinese dialect spoken in the Wenzhou region of China, and explores the potential of a Text-to-Speech (TTS) system as a tool for language preservation. The study employs UNESCO's language vitality assessment framework to conduct a comprehensive survey of Wenzhounese speakers, assessing factors such as intergenerational transmission, absolute number of speakers, and community attitudes. The findings suggest that while Wenzhounese is not immediately endangered, it faces significant challenges in intergenerational transmission and usage across diverse domains, placing it at risk of future endangerment. To explore innovative solutions for language preservation, the thesis develops a TTS system for Wenzhounese by fine-tuning a pre-trained Tacotron 2 model. Despite limitations in training data and the nascent stage of Wenzhounese TTS technology, the results demonstrate the feasibility of creating a functioning TTS system for the language. The Mean Opinion Score (MOS) evaluation of the synthesized speech highlights the need for further refinement and improvement.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Chen, K. (2024). Passing Voice to the Voiceless: An assessment of Wenzhounese Endangerment Level and a Text-to-Speech System for Language Preservation. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/dd556tj3400. https://doi.org/10.25740/dd556tj3400.",
